Thanks for checking. After reviewing both issues, mine is distinct:
- **#24083**: Only shows the error message without config details or root-cause analysis.
- **#24092**: A generic request to "adapt to DeepSeek V4" with no technical specifics.
My report provides verified workarounds that prove this is a bug in model ID recognition, not a missing feature.
**Workaround 1**: Using the legacy model ID `deepseek-reasoner` with `@ai-sdk/openai-compatible` works with identical config.
**Workaround 2** (via community member @svtter): Switch to `@ai-sdk/anthropic` with DeepSeek's Anthropic-compatible endpoint:
```json
{
  "provider": {
    "deepseek": {
      "npm": "@ai-sdk/anthropic",
      "name": "DeepSeek",
      "options": {
        "baseURL": "https://api.deepseek.com/anthropic",
        "apiKey": "<apikey>"
      },
      "models": {
        "deepseek-v4-pro": {
          "name": "DeepSeek-V4-Pro",
          "limit": { "context": 1048576, "output": 262144 },
          "options": {
            "thinking": { "type": "enabled", "budgetTokens": 8192 }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
Results:
- @ai-sdk/openai-compatible + deepseek-v4-pro = Broken
- @ai-sdk/openai-compatible + deepseek-reasoner = Works
- @ai-sdk/anthropic + deepseek-v4-pro = Works
This strongly suggests the bug is specifically in how OpenCode maps deepseek-v4-pro when using @ai-sdk/openai-compatible, not a general lack of V4 support.

